#lang ivy1.8

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MSI cache coherence over a split-transaction snooping bus:
# LIVENESS, with an arbitrary bus arbiter, under compassion for the arbiter.
#
# The model is `msi.ivy` (read that file first -- it documents the protocol
# and carries the safety proof) plus fairness instrumentation on the seven
# bus-side rules.  It proves
#
#       for every cache C:  globally ( waiting(C) -> eventually ~waiting(C) )
#
# i.e. every processor request -- a read miss, a write miss, or an upgrade --
# eventually receives its data/permission and the cache installs the line.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY COMPASSION, AND ONLY FOR THE ARBITER
#
# `bus_arbitrate(cl)` picks an *arbitrary* queued cache, and it needs an idle
# bus.  Under weak fairness the scheduler must offer `bus_arbitrate(_C)` a
# turn infinitely often, but it may offer every one of those turns while the
# bus is busy with somebody else.  Other caches can cycle request -> service
# -> request forever, the bus is idle only in the instants between finishing
# one transaction and starting the next, and the adversary simply never
# schedules _C's turn in one of those instants.  **The property is false under
# weak fairness alone** -- that is a property of an arbiter that does not
# remember who is waiting, not an artefact of the encoding.
#
# So for the arbiter, and only for the arbiter, we assume *compassion*
# (strong fairness):
#
#   if _C's request is available for picking infinitely often,
#   then the arbiter eventually picks it.
#
# This does not assume the conclusion.  The antecedent -- "the bus keeps
# becoming idle while _C is queued" -- still has to be discharged, and that is
# exactly what the lowest-order components [04]..[09] do.
#
# Every other rule needs only weak fairness, because its guard, once true,
# stays true until the rule itself fires:
#
#   send_snoop(C)     `to_snoop(C)` is cleared only here; the response that
#                     could occupy `snpchan(C)` is drained by [06]/[07]
#   snoop_respond(C)  a snoop in `snpchan(C)` is consumed only here
#   recv_ack(C)       an ack in `rspchan(C)` is consumed only here
#   bus_complete      nothing re-arms `pending_snoop` while the bus is busy
#   recv_dat_*(C)     a response in `snpchan(C)` is consumed only here
#
# Exactly one rule -- the arbiter -- fails this test, and it fails it because
# it is *parameterized by the cache*: another cache's pick is what falsifies
# `bus_cmd = no_req`.  `msi_fifo.ivy` exploits that asymmetry: it makes the
# arbiter unparameterized (serve the oldest request) and then plain weak
# fairness suffices.  The two results are incomparable -- weaker scheduler
# assumption there, but only about a bus that implements FIFO arbitration.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE PROOF
#
# A waiting cache is in exactly one of three stages ([waiting_stages]):
#
#   stage 1   reqchan(C) ~= no_req                 request queued at the bus
#   stage 2   bus_cmd ~= no_req & bus_owner = C    the bus is serving C
#   stage 3   snpchan(C) = dat_*                   response in flight to C
#
# Abbreviate the compassion antecedent
#
#     E  =  bus_cmd = no_req & reqchan(_C) ~= no_req
#
# One `ranking` call, ten lexicographic components, no auxiliary lemma:
#
#   [00],[01]  stage 1.  Following McMillan's `strongfair.ivy` idiom, the
#              symbolic tableau for E splits stage 1 into three cases:
#              E holds infinitely often  ->  [01], compassion delivers the
#              pick;  E holds finitely often but the last one has not happened
#              yet  ->  [00], where the tableau bit `eventually E` is itself
#              the ranking and must drop to false;  and E never happens again,
#              which is handled by [04]..[09] below.
#   [02],[03]  stage 3: _C installs the line it was sent.
#   [04]..[09] stage 2 -- and also the third case of stage 1.
#
# Why it has to be lexicographic rather than a flat set of stable schedulers
# (Rule 8 / `l2s_auto5`): the snoop-sweep rankings [04]..[09] are *grown* by
# `bus_arbitrate`, which starts a fresh transaction with every other cache
# owing it a snoop, and that happens freely while _C sits in stage 1 or stage
# 3.  Under Rule 10 that is legal, because in stage 1 one of [00]/[01] is
# scheduled and in stage 3 one of [02]/[03] is, so the sweep is preempted
# there and carries no conservation obligation.  Working out when no higher
# scheduler is active gives
#
#     ~pre([04]..[09])  /\  waiting(_C)
#       ==>  stage 2,  or  ( stage 1  and  ~(eventually E) )
#
# and both of those imply `bus_cmd ~= no_req`.  That is why every sweep
# ranking carries the conjunct `bus_cmd ~= no_req`: it makes them empty the
# instant the bus goes idle, so `bus_arbitrate` -- which needs an idle bus --
# can only fire from a state where they are preempted anyway.
#
# The third case of stage 1 is where an auxiliary lemma ("the bus always
# finishes its current transaction") would otherwise be needed.  Here it comes
# out for free: if E never happens again while _C's request is queued, then
# the bus is never idle again, so [04]..[09] are *required*, and they drive
# the bus to `bus_complete` -- which makes it idle.  Contradiction.
#
# Two facts about the `ranking` tactic that shape what is written below
# (see LIVENESS_CASE_STUDY_GERMAN.md §6.2, §6.3):
#
#   * only `l2s_needed_preserved` and `l2s_sched_stable` are discounted by
#     preemption.  `l2s_progress` is not, so *every* component must reduce its
#     own ranking whenever its own scheduler is on, preempted or not.  Since
#     the `wf_*` flags pulse even when their rule is disabled, each scheduler
#     below is literally the enabling guard of its rule.
#   * component suffixes are sorted as strings, so they are written with two
#     digits.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

finite type cache
type value

type busreq   = { no_req, busrd, busrdx, busupgr }
type snoopmsg = { no_snoop, snp_inv, snp_dgrade, dat_shared, dat_modified }
type rspmsg   = { no_rsp, snp_ack }
type cstate   = { invalid, shared, modified }

object s = {
    var reqchan(C:cache) : busreq
    var snpchan(C:cache) : snoopmsg
    var snpval(C:cache)  : value
    var rspchan(C:cache) : rspmsg

    var st(C:cache)  : cstate
    var val(C:cache) : value

    var bus_cmd   : busreq
    var bus_owner : cache
    var pending_snoop(C:cache) : bool
    var to_snoop(C:cache) : bool

    var mem_val : value
    var dirty   : bool

    var waiting(C:cache) : bool
}

var owner    : cache
var last_val : value

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Fairness instrumentation.
#
# Each bus-side rule gets a boolean flag pulsed (`:= true; := false;`) in a
# single atomic step, *before* the rule's guard is tested, and the rule itself
# becomes a guarded command.  A turn offered to a disabled rule is then a
# stutter step, so `globally eventually wf_x` says only "the scheduler offers
# rule x a turn infinitely often" -- a statement about the scheduler that is
# always satisfiable.
#
# The tempting alternative -- pulse the flag and then `require` the guard --
# is a vacuity trap: in an exported action `require` prunes the trace, so the
# assumption silently becomes "rule x *successfully executes* infinitely
# often", which is stronger than strong fairness and can be unsatisfiable.
# See LIVENESS_CASE_STUDY_GERMAN.md §3.1.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

var wf_arb(C:cache)   : bool            # bus_arbitrate: turn offered
var f_arb(C:cache)    : bool            # bus_arbitrate: actually fired
var wf_send(C:cache)  : bool            # send_snoop
var wf_snoop(C:cache) : bool            # snoop_respond
var wf_ack(C:cache)   : bool            # recv_ack
var wf_cmpl  : bool                     # bus_complete
var wf_rds(C:cache)   : bool            # recv_dat_shared
var wf_rdm(C:cache)   : bool            # recv_dat_modified

after init {
    s.reqchan(C) := no_req;
    s.snpchan(C) := no_snoop;
    s.rspchan(C) := no_rsp;
    s.st(C) := invalid;
    s.bus_cmd := no_req;
    s.pending_snoop(C) := false;
    s.to_snoop(C) := false;
    s.waiting(C) := false;
    s.dirty := false;
    s.mem_val := last_val;
    s.val(C) := last_val;
    s.snpval(C) := last_val;

    wf_arb(C) := false;
    f_arb(C) := false;
    wf_send(C) := false;
    wf_snoop(C) := false;
    wf_ack(C) := false;
    wf_cmpl := false;
    wf_rds(C) := false;
    wf_rdm(C) := false;

}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Processor side: environment actions, no fairness flag (we never want to
# force a processor to issue a request).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

action pr_read(cl:cache) = {
    require ~s.waiting(cl);
    require s.st(cl) = invalid & s.reqchan(cl) = no_req;
    s.reqchan(cl) := busrd;
    s.waiting(cl) := true;
}

action pr_write(cl:cache) = {
    require ~s.waiting(cl);
    require s.st(cl) ~= modified & s.reqchan(cl) = no_req;
    if s.st(cl) = shared {
        s.reqchan(cl) := busupgr;
    } else {
        s.reqchan(cl) := busrdx;
    };
    s.waiting(cl) := true;
}

action pr_store(cl:cache, v:value) = {
    require s.st(cl) = modified;
    s.val(cl) := v;
    last_val := v;
}

action evict_shared(cl:cache) = {
    require ~s.waiting(cl) & s.st(cl) = shared;
    s.st(cl) := invalid;
}

action evict_modified(cl:cache) = {
    require ~s.waiting(cl) & s.st(cl) = modified;
    s.mem_val := s.val(cl);
    s.dirty := false;
    s.st(cl) := invalid;
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Bus side: the seven fair rules.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# FIFO arbitration: serve the oldest queued request.  This is the only change
# to the protocol itself, and it is what replaces compassion.  Note that the
# rule is unparameterized, which is exactly what makes its guard stable.
action bus_arbitrate(cl:cache) = {
    wf_arb(cl) := true;
    wf_arb(cl) := false;
    if s.bus_cmd = no_req & s.reqchan(cl) ~= no_req {
        f_arb(cl) := true;
        f_arb(cl) := false;
        s.bus_cmd := s.reqchan(cl);
        s.reqchan(cl) := no_req;
        s.bus_owner := cl;
        s.pending_snoop(C) := C ~= cl;
        s.to_snoop(C) := C ~= cl;
    }
}

action send_snoop(cl:cache) = {
    wf_send(cl) := true;
    wf_send(cl) := false;
    if s.bus_cmd ~= no_req & s.to_snoop(cl) & s.snpchan(cl) = no_snoop {
        if s.bus_cmd = busrd {
            s.snpchan(cl) := snp_dgrade;
        } else {
            s.snpchan(cl) := snp_inv;
        };
        s.to_snoop(cl) := false;
    }
}

action snoop_respond(cl:cache) = {
    wf_snoop(cl) := true;
    wf_snoop(cl) := false;
    if (s.snpchan(cl) = snp_inv | s.snpchan(cl) = snp_dgrade)
       & s.rspchan(cl) = no_rsp {
        if s.st(cl) = modified {
            s.mem_val := s.val(cl);
            s.dirty := false;
        };
        if s.snpchan(cl) = snp_inv {
            s.st(cl) := invalid;
            if s.reqchan(cl) = busupgr {
                s.reqchan(cl) := busrdx;
            }
        } else {
            if s.st(cl) = modified {
                s.st(cl) := shared;
            }
        };
        s.snpchan(cl) := no_snoop;
        s.rspchan(cl) := snp_ack;
    }
}

action recv_ack(cl:cache) = {
    wf_ack(cl) := true;
    wf_ack(cl) := false;
    if s.bus_cmd ~= no_req & s.rspchan(cl) = snp_ack {
        s.pending_snoop(cl) := false;
        s.rspchan(cl) := no_rsp;
    }
}

action bus_complete = {
    wf_cmpl := true;
    wf_cmpl := false;
    if s.bus_cmd ~= no_req
       & (forall I. ~s.pending_snoop(I))
       & s.snpchan(s.bus_owner) = no_snoop {
        s.snpval(s.bus_owner) := s.mem_val;
        if s.bus_cmd = busrd {
            s.snpchan(s.bus_owner) := dat_shared;
        } else {
            s.snpchan(s.bus_owner) := dat_modified;
        };
        s.bus_cmd := no_req;
    }
}

action recv_dat_shared(cl:cache) = {
    wf_rds(cl) := true;
    wf_rds(cl) := false;
    if s.snpchan(cl) = dat_shared {
        s.st(cl) := shared;
        s.val(cl) := s.snpval(cl);
        s.snpchan(cl) := no_snoop;
        s.waiting(cl) := false;
    }
}

action recv_dat_modified(cl:cache) = {
    wf_rdm(cl) := true;
    wf_rdm(cl) := false;
    if s.snpchan(cl) = dat_modified {
        s.st(cl) := modified;
        s.val(cl) := s.snpval(cl);
        s.snpchan(cl) := no_snoop;
        s.dirty := true;
        owner := cl;
        s.waiting(cl) := false;
    }
}

export pr_read
export pr_write
export pr_store
export evict_shared
export evict_modified
export bus_arbitrate
export send_snoop
export snoop_respond
export recv_ack
export bus_complete
export recv_dat_shared
export recv_dat_modified

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The fairness flags are only momentarily true.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

invariant ~wf_arb(C)
invariant ~f_arb(C)
invariant ~wf_send(C)
invariant ~wf_snoop(C)
invariant ~wf_ack(C)
invariant ~wf_cmpl
invariant ~wf_rds(C)
invariant ~wf_rdm(C)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Coherence (proved in msi.ivy; unchanged here).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

invariant [swmr] s.st(C) = modified & C ~= D -> s.st(D) = invalid
invariant [dvi]  s.st(C) ~= invalid -> s.val(C) = last_val

invariant [mod_dirty]    s.st(C) = modified -> s.dirty & C = owner
invariant [dirty_owner]  s.dirty -> s.st(owner) = modified
invariant [shared_clean] s.st(C) = shared -> ~s.dirty
invariant [mem_clean]    ~s.dirty -> s.mem_val = last_val

invariant [pending_busy]    s.pending_snoop(C) -> s.bus_cmd ~= no_req
invariant [tosnoop_pending] s.to_snoop(C) -> s.pending_snoop(C)
invariant [ack_pending]     s.rspchan(C) = snp_ack -> s.pending_snoop(C) & ~s.to_snoop(C)
invariant [ack_no_snp]      s.rspchan(C) = snp_ack -> s.snpchan(C) = no_snoop
invariant [snp_pending]     (s.snpchan(C) = snp_inv | s.snpchan(C) = snp_dgrade)
                            -> s.pending_snoop(C) & ~s.to_snoop(C)

invariant [snp_is_dgrade]   s.bus_cmd = busrd -> s.snpchan(C) ~= snp_inv
invariant [snp_is_inv]      (s.bus_cmd = busrdx | s.bus_cmd = busupgr)
                            -> s.snpchan(C) ~= snp_dgrade

invariant [owner_free]
    s.bus_cmd ~= no_req -> ~s.pending_snoop(s.bus_owner) & s.snpchan(s.bus_owner) = no_snoop
                           & s.rspchan(s.bus_owner) = no_rsp

invariant [grant_pending]
    s.bus_cmd ~= no_req & (s.snpchan(C) = dat_shared | s.snpchan(C) = dat_modified)
    -> s.pending_snoop(C) & s.to_snoop(C)

invariant [not_stuck]
    s.pending_snoop(C) & ~s.to_snoop(C)
    -> s.snpchan(C) = snp_inv | s.snpchan(C) = snp_dgrade | s.rspchan(C) = snp_ack

invariant [one_grant]
    (s.snpchan(C) = dat_shared | s.snpchan(C) = dat_modified)
    & (s.snpchan(D) = dat_shared | s.snpchan(D) = dat_modified) -> C = D
invariant [grant_val]
    (s.snpchan(C) = dat_shared | s.snpchan(C) = dat_modified) -> s.snpval(C) = last_val
invariant [grant_clean]
    (s.snpchan(C) = dat_shared | s.snpchan(C) = dat_modified) -> ~s.dirty
invariant [grant_mod_excl]
    s.snpchan(C) = dat_modified & D ~= C -> s.st(D) = invalid

invariant [inv_progress]
    (s.bus_cmd = busrdx | s.bus_cmd = busupgr) & C ~= s.bus_owner
    & (~s.pending_snoop(C) | s.rspchan(C) = snp_ack) -> s.st(C) = invalid

invariant [mod_snooped]
    s.bus_cmd ~= no_req & s.st(C) = modified
    -> C ~= s.bus_owner & s.pending_snoop(C) & s.rspchan(C) ~= snp_ack

invariant [req_not_mod]   s.reqchan(C) ~= no_req -> s.st(C) ~= modified
invariant [upgr_shared]   s.reqchan(C) = busupgr -> s.st(C) = shared
invariant [busrd_inv]     s.reqchan(C) = busrd -> s.st(C) = invalid
invariant [owner_not_mod] s.bus_cmd ~= no_req -> s.st(s.bus_owner) ~= modified
invariant [upgr_owner]    s.bus_cmd = busupgr -> s.st(s.bus_owner) = shared

invariant [waiting_stages]
    s.waiting(C) <-> (s.reqchan(C) ~= no_req
                      | (s.bus_cmd ~= no_req & s.bus_owner = C)
                      | s.snpchan(C) = dat_shared
                      | s.snpchan(C) = dat_modified)

invariant [stage1_excl]
    s.reqchan(C) ~= no_req ->
        ~(s.bus_cmd ~= no_req & s.bus_owner = C)
        & s.snpchan(C) ~= dat_shared & s.snpchan(C) ~= dat_modified

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Fairness assumptions.
#
# Weak fairness (justice) for every bus-side rule: the scheduler offers each
# rule a turn infinitely often.  Because the flags are pulsed before the guard
# is tested, these are assumptions about the *scheduler*, not about the
# protocol -- they can always be satisfied, so they are not vacuous.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

explicit temporal axiom [wfa_cmpl]  globally eventually wf_cmpl
explicit temporal axiom [wfa_send]  forall C. globally eventually wf_send(C)
explicit temporal axiom [wfa_snoop] forall C. globally eventually wf_snoop(C)
explicit temporal axiom [wfa_ack]   forall C. globally eventually wf_ack(C)
explicit temporal axiom [wfa_rds]   forall C. globally eventually wf_rds(C)
explicit temporal axiom [wfa_rdm]   forall C. globally eventually wf_rdm(C)

# Weak fairness for the arbiter is stated for completeness but is *not* used
# by the proof: as explained in the header, it is too weak to rule out
# starvation when the arbiter picks an arbitrary queued cache.
explicit temporal axiom [wfa_arb]   forall C. globally eventually wf_arb(C)

# Compassion (strong fairness) for the bus arbiter: if _C's request is
# available for picking infinitely often, the arbiter eventually picks it.
explicit temporal axiom [sf_arb]
  forall C. (globally eventually (s.bus_cmd = no_req & s.reqchan(C) ~= no_req))
            -> (globally eventually f_arb(C))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LIVENESS: every processor request eventually receives a response.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

explicit temporal property [live]
  forall C. globally (s.waiting(C) -> eventually ~s.waiting(C))
proof {
  tactic skolemizenp;
  instantiate wfa_cmpl;
  instantiate wfa_send;
  instantiate wfa_snoop;
  instantiate wfa_ack;
  instantiate wfa_rds;
  instantiate wfa_rdm;
  instantiate sf_arb with C = _C;
  tactic ranking with {

    # ===== stage 1: _C's request is still queued at the bus ===============
    #
    # [00] E happens only finitely often, but the last E has not happened
    # yet.  The tableau bit (eventually E) is itself the ranking: it must drop
    # to false, after which case [01] is impossible and the sweep takes over.
    definition work_created[00](N:cache) = true
    definition work_needed[00](N:cache)  = eventually (s.bus_cmd = no_req
                                                       & s.reqchan(_C) ~= no_req)
    definition work_invar[00]            = s.waiting(_C)
    definition work_progress[00](N:cache)= ~(eventually (s.bus_cmd = no_req
                                                         & s.reqchan(_C) ~= no_req))
    definition work_helpful[00](N:cache) = ~(globally eventually (s.bus_cmd = no_req
                                                                  & s.reqchan(_C) ~= no_req))
                                           & (eventually (s.bus_cmd = no_req
                                                          & s.reqchan(_C) ~= no_req))

    # [01] E happens infinitely often, so by compassion the arbiter
    # eventually picks _C's request and empties reqchan(_C).
    definition work_created[01](N:cache) = true
    definition work_needed[01](N:cache)  = s.reqchan(_C) ~= no_req
    definition work_invar[01]            = s.waiting(_C)
    definition work_progress[01](N:cache)= f_arb(_C)
    definition work_helpful[01](N:cache) = (globally eventually (s.bus_cmd = no_req
                                                                 & s.reqchan(_C) ~= no_req))
                                           & s.reqchan(_C) ~= no_req

    # ===== stage 3: _C installs the line it was sent ======================
    definition work_created[02](N:cache) = true
    definition work_needed[02](N:cache)  = s.waiting(_C)
    definition work_invar[02]            = s.waiting(_C)
    definition work_progress[02](N:cache)= wf_rds(N)
    definition work_helpful[02](N:cache) = N = _C & s.snpchan(_C) = dat_shared

    definition work_created[03](N:cache) = true
    definition work_needed[03](N:cache)  = s.waiting(_C)
    definition work_invar[03]            = s.waiting(_C)
    definition work_progress[03](N:cache)= wf_rdm(N)
    definition work_helpful[03](N:cache) = N = _C & s.snpchan(_C) = dat_modified

    # ===== stage 2 (and stage 1 with ~eventually E): the snoop sweep ======
    #
    #   pending_snoop --send_snoop--> snoop in snpchan
    #                 --snoop_respond--> ack in rspchan
    #                 --recv_ack--> obligation retired
    #
    # Each component ranks on a superset of the stages still to come, so the
    # rule that advances a cache through the sweep conserves every ranking
    # above it.  All of them carry `bus_cmd ~= no_req` -- see the header.

    # [04] the bus retires one snoop obligation.
    definition work_created[04](N:cache) = true
    definition work_needed[04](N:cache)  = s.bus_cmd ~= no_req
                                           & s.pending_snoop(N)
    definition work_invar[04]            = s.waiting(_C)
    definition work_progress[04](N:cache)= wf_ack(N)
    definition work_helpful[04](N:cache) = s.bus_cmd ~= no_req
                                           & s.rspchan(N) = snp_ack

    # [05] a bystander applies its snoop and acks.
    definition work_created[05](N:cache) = true
    definition work_needed[05](N:cache)  = s.bus_cmd ~= no_req
                                           & s.pending_snoop(N)
                                           & s.rspchan(N) ~= snp_ack
    definition work_invar[05]            = s.waiting(_C)
    definition work_progress[05](N:cache)= wf_snoop(N)
    definition work_helpful[05](N:cache) = (s.snpchan(N) = snp_inv
                                            | s.snpchan(N) = snp_dgrade)
                                           & s.rspchan(N) = no_rsp

    # [06] the bus puts the snoop in the bystander's channel.
    definition work_created[06](N:cache) = true
    definition work_needed[06](N:cache)  = s.bus_cmd ~= no_req
                                           & s.to_snoop(N)
    definition work_invar[06]            = s.waiting(_C)
    definition work_progress[06](N:cache)= wf_send(N)
    definition work_helpful[06](N:cache) = s.bus_cmd ~= no_req
                                           & s.to_snoop(N)
                                           & s.snpchan(N) = no_snoop

    # [07] a bystander drains a stale shared response, unblocking its snoop
    #      channel.  This is a genuine sweep stage: an unconsumed response
    #      occupies the very channel the next snoop has to travel down.
    definition work_created[07](N:cache) = true
    definition work_needed[07](N:cache)  = s.bus_cmd ~= no_req
                                           & s.pending_snoop(N)
                                           & s.snpchan(N) = dat_shared
    definition work_invar[07]            = s.waiting(_C)
    definition work_progress[07](N:cache)= wf_rds(N)
    definition work_helpful[07](N:cache) = s.bus_cmd ~= no_req
                                           & s.pending_snoop(N)
                                           & s.snpchan(N) = dat_shared

    # [08] likewise for a stale exclusive response.
    definition work_created[08](N:cache) = true
    definition work_needed[08](N:cache)  = s.bus_cmd ~= no_req
                                           & s.pending_snoop(N)
                                           & s.snpchan(N) = dat_modified
    definition work_invar[08]            = s.waiting(_C)
    definition work_progress[08](N:cache)= wf_rdm(N)
    definition work_helpful[08](N:cache) = s.bus_cmd ~= no_req
                                           & s.pending_snoop(N)
                                           & s.snpchan(N) = dat_modified

    # [09] every bystander has acked: answer the requester, release the bus.
    definition work_created[09](N:cache) = true
    definition work_needed[09](N:cache)  = s.bus_cmd ~= no_req
    definition work_invar[09]            = s.waiting(_C)
    definition work_progress[09](N:cache)= wf_cmpl
    definition work_helpful[09](N:cache) = s.bus_cmd ~= no_req
                                           & (forall I. ~s.pending_snoop(I))
                                           & s.snpchan(s.bus_owner) = no_snoop

    # The compassion assumption, restated as a state invariant of the
    # tableau.  `instantiate sf_arb` only pins it at the initial state, but
    # `l2s_progress_eventually[01]` is a postcondition checked at every state.
    # The lift is sound because []<>p is insensitive to finite prefixes, and
    # it is inductive from the local tableau constraints -- Ivy proves it, you
    # just have to ask.  (McMillan's `strongfair.ivy` carries the same line.)
    invariant (globally eventually (s.bus_cmd = no_req & s.reqchan(_C) ~= no_req))
              -> (globally eventually f_arb(_C))
  }
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The same property in its literal phrasing: a request queued on the bus is
# eventually answered.
#
# This is a corollary of [live], chained propositionally (Rule 7): by
# [waiting_stages] a queued request implies `waiting`, and `eventually
# ~waiting(_C)` then discharges the goal.  The ranking here is degenerate --
# `work_needed = true` never shrinks -- and the obligation is met instead
# because `work_invar` and `work_progress` are contradictory: the tableau bit
# `eventually ~waiting(_C)` supplied by [live] cannot coexist with the goal
# never happening.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

explicit temporal property [request_answered]
  forall C. globally (s.reqchan(C) ~= no_req -> eventually ~s.waiting(C))
proof {
  tactic skolemizenp;
  instantiate live with C = _C;
  tactic ranking with {
    definition work_created = true
    definition work_needed  = true
    definition work_invar   = eventually ~s.waiting(_C)
    definition work_progress= ~s.waiting(_C)
    definition work_helpful = true
  }
}
